<section class="hero">
  <div class="text">
    <h1>Leadership Fluency: How to Lead When AI Does (Almost) Everything Else</h1>
    <p>A daylong workshop that helps leaders untangle the four roles of leadership, and consciously choose between leading, managing, directing, and coaching to move people and work forward.</p>

    <p>If you’d like a deeper dive into the framework, I wrote an <%= link_to('introductory article on my blog', '/words-make-worlds/how-to-lead-when-ai-does-almost-everything/') %>.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<hr />


<section class="content">
  <div class="col-1">
    <h2>Why This Workshop?</h2>
  </div>

  <div class="col-2">
    <blockquote>
      <p>Exploring the four modes of leadership gave me the clarity I’d been missing. It’s a fantastic framework, I can’t wait to begin applying it within our org.</p>
    </blockquote>

    <h3>When you step into a leadership role you take on four distinct areas of responsibility: holding and communicating the vision, defining outcomes, running the business, and developing talent.</h3>

    <p>In practice, this all blends together. It's hard to see where your energy really goes, or which of the four areas you spend the most time in.</p>

    <p>The leader focuses on vision without clarifying clear targets, the director focuses on outcomes but doesn’t connect them with the larger purpose, the operational leader excells at process but falls short when it comes to people, and the people-oriented leader is great at building trust but can’t seem to drive results.</p>

    <p>Whatever your strengths and leadership comfort zone, this workshop helps you map your complete leadership landscape to get clear on what you spend time on and what could use more focus. With that clarity, you can make conscious choices about how you want to lead rather than operating from habit or overwhelm.</p>
  </div>

</section>

<hr />

<%= partial "fluency_webinar" %>

<hr />

<section class="content">
  <div class="col-1">
    <h2>Who this is for</h2>
  </div>

  <div class="col-2">
    <blockquote>
      <p>The framework we learned about in the course is truly useful—it helps establish structure around your leadership approach, enabling you to communicate with clarity, adapt to different situations, and inspire your team with confidence and consistency. Can only recommend.</p>
    </blockquote>

    <p><strong>This workshop is designed for folks who have at least some leadership experience under their belt, whether in a management track or IC track (staff, principal, etc.).</strong></p>

    <p>Whatever your seniority, you’ve started realize that the way you lead isn’t always producing the results you want. You know there have to be better ways, both in your own approach, and how you integrate AI into your leaership practice.</p>

    <p>The solution isn’t necessarily in better goals, a different management style, or more agents, but in choosing the right role for each situation. When leaders have the fluency to step into leading, directing, managing, and coaching as needed, conversations are transformed and results follow.</p>

    <p>This workshop is for leaders who:</p>

    <ul class="bullets">
      <li>Have responsibility for teams, projects, or functions (at any level)</li>
      <li>Feel exhausted from managing everything by themselves</li>
      <li>Want more capacity to have focused, purposeful conversations</li>
      <li>Are navigating situations that require flexibility and creativity</li>
      <li>Are willing to work with real leadership challenges</li>
    </ul>

    <p>Whether you’re newer to leadership or a seasoned executive deepening your craft, this workshop brings visibility to how how you lead and expands what is possible.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<hr />

<section class="content">
  <div class="col-1">
    <h2>What you can expect</h2>
  </div>

  <div class="col-2">
    <blockquote>
      <p>The leadership fluency visualization was a game-changer, and I really appreciated the interactive nature of the course—practicing with both real-life and hypothetical scenarios made the concepts stick. Highly recommend!!</p>
    </blockquote>

    <p><strong>This workshop combines experiential learning with conceptual frameworks to help you expand your leadership range in a supported environment.</strong></p>

    <p>You’ll work with real leadership challenges—both your own and throught case studies—to understand the distinctions between leading, directing, managing, and coaching. Through hands-on practice you’ll get familiar with the strengths of reach role, building practical understanding that goes beyond just theory.</p>

    <p>Using structured exercises and reflection, you’ll map your current challenges to see your current leadership “shape” and identify opportunities for growth. You’ll get a sense for the conversations that feel most natural to you and where to expand your range. This includes developing awareness of the body's relationship to the four leadership roles. For most leaders the somatic dimension isn’t unnoticed but deeply influences how they lead.</p>

    <p>We’ll also work to distinguish where your human leadership skills are non-negotiable, and where AI can play a role in increasing leadership and team efficacy.</p>

    <p>You’ll leave with leadership practices designed for daily use—ways to continue building your fluency without requiring separate development time—and a clearer awareness of your strengths, edges, and the conscious choices available to you.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<hr />

<section class="content">
  <div class="col-1">
    <h2>Syllabus</h2>
  </div>

  <div class="col-2">

    <blockquote>
      <p>I learned a highly practical leadership framework that I can directly apply in my role. The workshop was engaging, interactive, and full of actionable insights. I would highly recommend this course.</p>
    </blockquote>

    <h3>Pre-work</h3>
    <p>20 - 30 minute reflective practice to surface the dimensions of your current leadership work.</p>

    <h3>Workshop 1: The Four Roles of Leadership</h3>
    <p>Leadership is more complex than any single style or approach can contain. In this foundational workshop, you’ll be introduced to the Leadership Fluency Framework—a map of four distinct roles that every leader navigates: LEAD, DIRECT, MANAGE, and COACH. </p>

    <p>We explore these four roles as distinct stances, each with its own mindset, posture, and conversation type, each connecting different dimensions of organizational life.</p>

    <p>Through reflection, small group work, and somatic exploration, you’ll develop an intuitive feel for what each role looks and feels like in practice. You'll also have a chance to apply this to your own leadership practice to understand what each role looks like <em>for you.</em></p>

    <p>You leave with a clear picture of your current leadership shape—and a sense of where you want it to go.</p>

    <h3>Workshop 2: Leadership Fluency in Action</h3>
    <p>Now that you are familiar with the roles, Workshop 2 gives you an opportunity to apply them. You’ll bring the framework into contact with the real challenges, conversations, and organizational dynamics you navigate every day.</p>

    <p>We’ll explore how the shape of your own leadership, learn how conversation types can help you read a situation, and practice consciously choosing the role(s) a situation calls for.</p>

    <p>We’ll look at how pairs of roles lock together to create an organizational model that drives strategy, execution, structure, relationships, and culture, as well as how to manage the tensions between them so your organization stays in motion.</p>

    <p>We’ll also zoom out to the team level, examining how leadership fluency shows up collectively and how your leadership team can work with the framework together.</p>

    <p>The workshop closes with reflection and a practical next-steps design, so you leave with real commitments for where you're taking your practice.</p>

  </div>
</section>

